#       "interaction_history": "[t_01] interviewer: Walk me through how you'd structure your verification strategy combining Foundry and Certora, and how you'd handle a gas optimization involving inline assembly or bit-packing.\n[t_02] candidate: So on the base layer I'd lean on found-ry doing random call sequences - fast, cheap in variant fuzzing that catches regressions quick, but it can't mathematically prove nothing's wrong since it's just sampling a slice of the state space.\n[t_03] interviewer: And for the stronger guarantee?\n[t_04] candidate: For that I'd bring in sir tora prover - it uses a S M T solver to do actual mathematical induction, a base case and an inductive step, so the property holds no matter the call order, which is way past what random fuzzing alone gives you.\n[t_05] interviewer: How would you bridge the two tools together?\n[t_06] candidate: There's a way to map your existing found-ry invariant assertions straight into the sir tora specs written in see-vee-el, so you're not rewriting everything twice from scratch.\n[t_07] interviewer: And on the gas side, say someone wants to introduce heavy inline assembly and bit-packed state variables to save gas?\n[t_08] candidate: That's going to hurt us on the verification side - assembly kind of steps outside Solidity's type safety and hides the control flow, so when the solver tries to reason about it, it either times out or can't fully verify the path. And packing multiple values into one slot means you're doing manual bit-shifting by hand, which opens up its own overflow-style bugs and makes the invariants way messier to write cleanly in the spec language.\n[t_09] candidate: Before any of that though, I'd probably run it through a general shadow-check pass first, just as a sanity thing, to catch anything obvious."
